        <div id="creatine-evidence" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. What the Creatine Evidence Actually Says
          </h2>

          <p>
            The International Society of Sports Nutrition's 2017 position stand on creatine, led by Richard Kreider, concludes that creatine monohydrate is the most effective nutritional supplement currently available for increasing high-intensity exercise capacity and lean body mass during training. That is a strong statement from a cautious body, and it rests on hundreds of controlled trials across several decades rather than a handful of small studies.
          </p>

          <p>
            The mechanism is straightforward. Creatine raises the store of phosphocreatine in muscle, which your body uses to regenerate ATP during short, hard efforts like a heavy set or a sprint. With fuller stores, most people can squeeze out an extra rep or two across a workout. Over weeks and months, that extra work adds up to a little more training stimulus, which is where the gains in strength and lean mass come from. Creatine does not build muscle on its own; it helps you train slightly harder.
          </p>

          <p>
            The dosing guidance is simple. The position stand describes two routes. You can load with about 0.3 g per kg of body weight per day, roughly 20 g split into four doses, for five to seven days, and then drop to 3 to 5 g a day. Or you can skip loading and take 3 to 5 g a day from the start; muscle stores reach the same saturation in about three to four weeks. For a blend in a daily shake, the second route is the only one that makes sense, because loading would mean four shakes a day.
          </p>

          <p>
            Timing matters much less than consistency. Studies comparing pre- and post-workout creatine have found small differences at most, and the practical consensus is to take it at whatever time you will remember. There is some evidence that taking creatine with carbohydrate and protein modestly increases how much muscle retains, which is one honest argument for putting it in a shake. It is not a large effect, and it applies equally whether the creatine came pre-mixed or from a separate tub.
          </p>

          <p>
            Creatine works for most people, but not identically. People who eat little meat or fish tend to start with lower stores and often respond more, which makes creatine particularly relevant for vegetarians and vegans. Some people respond very little. The ISSN and later reviews also note that monohydrate remains the form with the evidence behind it; creatine HCl, buffered creatine and creatine ethyl ester have not been shown to work better, and usually cost more.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="effective-dose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Does Each Product Hit an Effective Dose?
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is where pre-mixed products divide into two groups. Orgain 30g Protein + Creatine, Sunwarrior Protein + Creatine, Myprotein Clear Whey Isolate + Creatine and the US label of Optimum Nutrition Serious Mass all list 5 g of creatine monohydrate per serving, the top of the standard maintenance range. One serving a day covers you completely, and you never have to think about it again.
          </p>

          <p>
            MuscleTech Nitro-Tech and Vega Protein + Creatine list 3 g. That is the bottom of the effective range, and the ISSN position stand describes 3 g a day as enough to saturate muscle stores over about a month. For most people of average size, 3 g is fine. Larger lifters, roughly 90 kg and up, may prefer closer to 5 g, and some researchers suggest scaling maintenance to around 0.03 g per kg of body weight, which puts a 100 kg lifter at 3 g as well. Either way, 3 g is a real dose, not a sprinkle.
          </p>

          <p>
            The trouble starts when you use these powders the way people actually use protein powder. Many lifters have two shakes on training days and none on rest days. With a creatine blend, that means 6 to 10 g on some days and nothing on others. The extra creatine is not dangerous for healthy adults, but it is wasted money, and the rest days without any creatine slow down how quickly your stores fill. Creatine rewards daily consistency, not big doses.
          </p>

          <p>
            The opposite problem happens with half servings. Someone who uses one scoop of Orgain instead of the full two-scoop serving, to save calories, gets 2.5 g of creatine. That is still not useless over time, but it falls under the range the research uses. Serious Mass is the extreme case: the 5 g figure is per full serving of roughly 1,250 calories, and many people sensibly start with a half serving, which halves the creatine.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, read the label for the form and the number, every time. Creatine monohydrate and micronized creatine monohydrate are the same molecule; micronized just dissolves more easily. Creapure, listed on the Myprotein product, is a branded monohydrate made in Germany with tight purity specs. Anything that only lists a creatine blend or matrix without a gram figure for monohydrate should go back on the shelf.
          </p>
        </div>

