        <div id="mix-at-home" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. How to Mix Your Own Protein and Creatine Shake
          </h2>

          <p>
            Mixing at home is about as simple as supplements get. Add one serving of your usual protein powder and 3 to 5 g of creatine monohydrate, roughly one level teaspoon of unflavored powder, to water or milk, shake and drink. Unflavored creatine has almost no taste, so it disappears into any chocolate or vanilla shake. Micronized creatine dissolves more fully and leaves less grit at the bottom of the bottle.
          </p>

          <p>
            Creatine is stable as a dry powder for years, but it slowly breaks down to creatinine in liquid, faster when the liquid is warm or acidic. A shake you mix and drink within a few hours is fine. Do not batch a week of pre-mixed shakes in the fridge, and do not leave a creatine shake in a hot car. If you like to prepare drinks ahead, keep the creatine in a small container and add it when you mix.
          </p>

          <p>
            You can also stir creatine into almost anything else: coffee, juice, yogurt, oatmeal or a smoothie. Hot coffee is fine if you drink it in the normal time frame; the breakdown in a single cup is small. If your shake is a smoothie with fruit, our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-smoothies/">best protein powder for smoothies</a> guide covers which bases blend cleanly. Creatine does not need to go in your protein shake at all; it only needs to go in you, daily.
          </p>

          <p>
            The real advantage of mixing your own is control. On rest days, when you may skip the shake, you still take your 5 g in a glass of water. When you have two shakes, you add creatine to one. If you change protein, because a flavor runs out or you switch to a <a href="/best-whey-protein-concentrate/">cheaper whey concentrate</a>, your creatine routine does not change. And if you ever want to stop creatine, for a blood test or any other reason, you simply stop adding it.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you choose this route, buy plain creatine monohydrate rather than a flavored blend, and look for a third-party seal if you compete. Skip anything marketed as an advanced or buffered form unless you have a specific reason; the evidence base is built on plain monohydrate, and that is what the cheap tubs contain.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="when-separate" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. When Buying Separately Is the Better Call
          </h2>

          <p>
            For most readers, separate tubs are the better default, and there are several situations where we would push you firmly that way. The first is if you drink more than one protein shake a day or use protein powder in cooking. Every additional scoop of a blend adds another dose of creatine, and protein pancakes or baked oats made with a creatine blend are an expensive way to get a second dose you did not need.
          </p>

          <p>
            The second is if your protein intake varies with your schedule. People who use protein powder only on training days, or only when they miss a meal, will get creatine on an inconsistent schedule. Creatine works by keeping muscle stores topped up, and that requires daily intake. A separate tub you take with breakfast every day solves this; a blend tied to your shake habit does not.
          </p>

          <p>
            Third, choose separate tubs if you are particular about your protein. The creatine blends cover a narrow slice of the market: one mainstream whey blend, one clear whey, three plant blends and a gainer. If you want an unflavored isolate, a lactose-free option, a grass-fed whey or a specific flavor you love, you will almost certainly have to give it up to get creatine pre-mixed. Our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-muscle-gain/">muscle gain picks</a> cover a much wider range of proteins to pair with plain creatine.
          </p>

          <p>
            Fourth, separate is better for tested athletes. A certified protein and a certified creatine are easy to find. A blend with the same certification is rare; among the products here, only Vega lists Informed Choice. If you are subject to drug testing, check the certification database for the exact product and lot rather than relying on a front-of-tub claim.
          </p>

          <p>
            The blends make sense in narrower cases. If you drink exactly one shake a day, every day, a 5 g blend is genuinely simpler. If you are a plant-based eater, the Orgain and Sunwarrior blends are competitive on cost and convenient. And if you keep a tub at the office or in a gym bag, one tub beats two. Those are real advantages; they are just not universal ones.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Safety, Water Weight and Who Should Skip Creatine
          </h2>

          <p>
            Creatine monohydrate has one of the best safety records of any supplement, and the ISSN position stand found no evidence of harm in healthy people at recommended doses, including in studies lasting several years. Still, this is general information, not medical advice. If you have kidney disease, take medication that affects the kidneys, are pregnant or breastfeeding, or are under 18, speak to your doctor before adding creatine.
          </p>

          <p>
            Expect a little water weight. Creatine draws water into muscle cells, and many people gain somewhere around 1 to 2 kg in the first weeks, more with a loading phase. This is water inside the muscle, not fat or bloating under the skin, and it levels off. It can matter if you compete in a weight class or are tracking a weight-loss goal closely, so plan for it rather than being surprised by the scale.
          </p>

          <p>
            Creatine raises blood creatinine, a marker that doctors use to estimate kidney function. That does not mean creatine harms the kidneys, but it can make a routine blood test look worse than it is. Tell your doctor you take creatine, and if a test result is borderline, ask whether to pause it for a few weeks and retest. This is one practical reason to keep creatine separate: you can stop it without changing your protein.
          </p>

          <p>
            Other worries come up often but have weak support. Studies have not found that creatine causes cramping or dehydration in healthy athletes. The hair loss concern traces back to one 2009 study in rugby players that found a rise in DHT; it did not measure hair loss and has not been replicated. Some people get stomach upset from large single doses, which is another reason to stick to 3 to 5 g rather than doubling up with two blended shakes.
          </p>

          <p>
            For the protein side, the usual cautions apply. Read our <a href="/is-protein-powder-safe/">protein powder safety guide</a> for heavy metals, sweeteners and allergens. Whey blends contain milk, and the mass gainer on this list is very high in fast carbohydrate, which is a poor fit for anyone managing blood sugar.
          </p>
        </div>

