              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Paying for fancy creatine forms</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Creatine HCl, buffered creatine and creatine ethyl ester usually cost more and have not been shown to beat plain monohydrate. The ISSN position stand and the studies behind it use creatine monohydrate. Micronized monohydrate and Creapure are fine; they are still monohydrate, just finer or held to tighter purity standards.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Pre-mixing shakes days ahead</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Creatine slowly breaks down to creatinine once it is dissolved, faster in warm or acidic liquid. A shake you drink within a few hours is fine, but a week of pre-mixed bottles in the fridge loses some potency. Keep powder dry until you mix it, and add creatine when you make the shake rather than in advance.
              </p>
